= Influence

== Robustness

=== The say-on-pay effect survives every alternative the paper tries.

- The obvious worry is that ISS simply picks the plans shareholders would have rejected anyway.

- The paper deals with that in three ways, and I will give you each one.

- First, it compares meetings just above and just below the ISS pay-for-performance cutoff, where the plans are nearly identical; the gap is still 22 points.

- Second, it drops every firm with a prior failed vote, so no plan is already notorious; the gap is 24 points.

- Third, it uses Glass Lewis recommendations on the same plans as a placebo, and they move support by only 6 points.

- So the effect is not an artifact of selection.
